#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059670 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059436 :question/id #uuid "a0969b02-52c8-5d22-b3e0-b594d9fbd4d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-13413" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d12a03b0100b460301fa0a235ce73be995594091fdace654b5a3b9ada6aa259e.png)\n\n \n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=f(2x)-1?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=f(2x)-1$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis, and then\n\n- translating it by $1$ unit down.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the local maximum of $y=f(2x)-1.$ So, we need to find the image of $(2,6),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y = f(2x)-1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,6).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the result along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and get the graph of $y=f(2x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 2, 6 \\right) = (1,6).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit downward and get the graph of $y = f(2x)-1.$ After this transformation, the point $(1,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,6-1) = (1,5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=f(2x)-1$ is $(1,5).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048164 :topic/math-academy-id 1888 :topic/title "Global Extrema of Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050033 :topic/math-academy-id 2707 :topic/title "Local Extrema of Functions"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060004 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0737c6dd-2968-5cc9-a8f8-c0079a8268a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253724} {:answer/id #uuid "2173f031-3969-54c9-823a-49ff47d49386" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253967} {:answer/id #uuid "75bbd111-a6df-57f9-ac2f-45af1364cecd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254712} {:answer/id #uuid "8398ba0c-a692-519d-9e74-4243e928998b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254841} {:answer/id #uuid "acc2456f-4e14-5655-aa35-0e7518c63750" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2173f031-3969-54c9-823a-49ff47d49386" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253967} :answer-field/id #uuid "7037b1e7-eb89-5813-ae66-13b1ef312f28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256125}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bce89a1c-1788-5140-a53a-cfabf6eebe67" :question/math-academy-id "q-121221" :question/problem "![](images/1e/1eb29eb3eb3627e24a23d7315295a1d889308d67895f9b798dbf9dd71ba226df.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=f(x-2)+1?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=f(x-2)+1$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $2$ units to the right, and then\n\n- translating it by $1$ unit up.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the local maximum of $y=f(x-2)+1.$ So, we need to find the image of $(2,3),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=f(x-2)+1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,3).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=f(x-2).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2+2,3)=(4,3).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit upward and get the graph of $y=f(x-2)+1.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,3+1)=(4,4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=f(x-2)+1$ is $(4,4).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060177 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "132ada2d-225c-55ef-ad34-507a30f3f6af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284958} {:answer/id #uuid "874eb524-3ed8-5726-b354-81594d6ba038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286028} {:answer/id #uuid "8da77226-b79f-5aa1-9fbb-eff1ddcce302" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,8)." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286083} {:answer/id #uuid "de9a3fa5-07d3-5f60-9264-81029b284219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286813} {:answer/id #uuid "e77beded-c213-529c-9af4-9172511b2a92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,8)." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "132ada2d-225c-55ef-ad34-507a30f3f6af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284958} :answer-field/id #uuid "e490dbf9-e5ff-5dfa-a559-074583566e32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287616}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c0bb866-f5d6-5c26-9148-409419fa5279" :question/math-academy-id "q-186866" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f4da78b7a12ed475b64c1f824e8cecdf36657c6d174f0db770219fd8dd6c0801.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of $y=-2f(x+1)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=-2f(x+1)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $1$ unit to the left, then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, and finally\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global **maximum** of $y=f(x)$ to the global **minimum** of $y=-2f(x+1).$ So, we need to find the image of $(-1,4),$ the global maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-2f(x+1),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-1,4).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1-1,4)=(-2,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $2$ along the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=2f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2,2\\cdot 4)=(-2,8).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we flip the result vertically across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-2f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,8)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2,-1\\cdot 8)=(-2,-8).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=-2f(x+1)$ is $(-2,-8).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233626 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "626e7d97-1fd4-5fea-b684-7fc6a67422d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-211287" :question/problem "![](images/28/282e65324ea1e7893da25ead1cc822c7c166df880c3aecc60face9adbbb7279a.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=f(x+3)-2?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=f(x+3)-2$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $3$ units to the left, and then\n\n- translating it by $2$ unit down.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the local maximum of $y=f(x+3)-2.$ So, we need to find the image of $(-2,6),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=f(x+3)-2,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-2,6).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $3$ units to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x+3).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2-3,6)=(-5,6).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units downward and get the graph of $y=f(x+3)-2.$ After this transformation, the point $(-5,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-5,6-2)=(-5,4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=f(x+3)-2$ is $(-5,4).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233627 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e205c358-cf42-5b44-9f76-2e404dd4a719" :question/math-academy-id "q-211304" :question/problem "![](images/73/73da11bf77a8bc10c00f5636176321ed1b44bbf614c8d4658e574e8ae5f85831.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of $y=f(3x)+4?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=f(3x)+4$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis, and then\n\n- translating it by $4$ units up.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global minimum of $y=f(x)$ to the global minimum of $y=f(3x)+4.$ So, we need to find the image of $(15,-5),$ the global minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=f(3x)+4,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(15,-5).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the result along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ and get the graph of $y=f(3x).$ After this transformation, the point $(15,-5)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 15,-5)=(5,-5).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the graph vertically by $4$ units up and get the graph of $y=f(3x)+4.$ After this transformation, the point $(5,-5)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(5,-5+4)=(5,-1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=f(3x)+4$ is $(5,-1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233628 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a903e74e-e530-53d5-8ad7-3ef2b34613b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-211305" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d8d23cf36bc9fb526c0e6d041b72e6b378638f662b67aae63531c66b4a8cd6c5.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2})$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis, and then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the local maximum of $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2}).$ So, we need to find the image of $(-2,-1),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2}),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-2,-1).$\n\n- Then, we  stretch the graph  along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $2$ and get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{x}{2}).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2\\cdot (-2),-1)=(-4,-1).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we stretch the result  along the $y$-axis by a scale factor of $3$ and get the graph of $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2}).$ After this transformation, the point $(-4,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-4,3\\cdot (-1))=(-4,-3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2})$ is $(-4,-3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233629 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e70b1b4-1844-5d99-88a9-0a513b5d410f" :question/math-academy-id "q-211461" :question/problem "![](images/57/57f2d73dd902d3e0bafec168cfe6ecd46dfc7d7a9e05f3cf33d710ca3f6b1e76.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of $y=2f(x)+5?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=2f(x)+5$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, and then\n\n- translating it by $5$ units up.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global minimum of $y=f(x)$ to the global minimum of $y=2f(x)+5.$ So, we need to find the image of $(4,-5),$ the global minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=2f(x)+5,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(4,-5).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph along the $y$-axis by a scale factor of $2$ and get the graph of $y=2f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,-5)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,2\\cdot (-5))=(4,-10).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result parallel to the $y$-axis by $5$ units up and get the graph of $y=2f(x)+5.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,-10)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,-10+5)=(4,-5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=2f(x)+5$ is $(4,-5).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290285 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d268d2d-7fd3-5193-ace2-27766687167e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288329} {:answer/id #uuid "4eb982b3-78f3-5bb2-88d8-08e1c9cc7f14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288576} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b214ed-c977-5da8-9b98-583c8599dbc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289314} {:answer/id #uuid "cc01c367-7b62-52ca-b3f4-660a5da6d436" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289386} {:answer/id #uuid "f0cb2914-db63-5a0a-b325-34eb1a5b726a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d268d2d-7fd3-5193-ace2-27766687167e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288329} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd3166ba-184a-5116-9d35-4f34e933136f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290176}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e2903fa-7395-523d-a538-0dfcbb4ba792" :question/math-academy-id "q-211288" :question/problem "![](images/09/09193a765398280ec967a89b97b6dbdf2fb0700e0bb0d8157f966cda5c7534f9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $5$ units to the right, and then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global minimum of $y=f(x)$ to the global minimum of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5).$ So, we need to find the image of $(-2,-4),$ the global minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-2,-4).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $5$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=f(x-5).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2+5,-4)=(3,-4).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we stretch the result along the $y$-axis by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ and get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-4))=(3,-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5)$ is $(3,-2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290286 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f3c3352-f7de-5a7e-9ebf-a00ce3258c8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288233} {:answer/id #uuid "36e46a0f-bc76-591c-9024-26e2124e639d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288401} {:answer/id #uuid "8f9c091e-f909-5d4d-b33c-8f35d5362696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288994} {:answer/id #uuid "c05a121d-0bcc-57a1-b95d-487aa2df06cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289304} {:answer/id #uuid "f7d7b031-0b65-5919-b36d-dfa7ed53d491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f3c3352-f7de-5a7e-9ebf-a00ce3258c8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288233} :answer-field/id #uuid "62e9b6f7-13d8-5a6b-800b-ea453f3a0704" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289941}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bba06ed1-401d-5f06-b7c3-1e90cd37f0df" :question/math-academy-id "q-211303" :question/problem "![](images/ff/ff2956aa2885a0abddd614a11e632d3dfd1114391a9d7e27d790746c159e7f79.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=-3f(x)-1?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=-3f(x)-1$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis,\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis, and finally\n\n- translating it by $1$ unit down.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local **minimum** of $y=f(x)$ to the local **maximum** of $y=-3f(x)-1.$ So, we need to find the image of $(4,-1),$ the local minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-3f(x)-1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(4,-1).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $3$ along the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=3f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,3\\cdot (-1))=(4,-3).\n$$\n\n- Next, we flip the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-3f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,-3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,-1\\cdot (-3))=(4,3).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we translate the result by $1$ unit down and get the graph of $y=-3f(x)-1.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,3-1)=(4,2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=-3f(x)-1$ is $(4,2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290287 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01b3ee48-738d-5154-93f4-593da455f6f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288038} {:answer/id #uuid "02202242-35fc-558e-b9c0-5e30f23f7048" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288042} {:answer/id #uuid "7017f50c-dcb4-516e-bbbc-55369bab2f30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288810} {:answer/id #uuid "c4904f95-4b3c-5515-9fcf-0a99b245182b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289330} {:answer/id #uuid "cbce72d8-a3b3-5305-909c-46fd579eba75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7017f50c-dcb4-516e-bbbc-55369bab2f30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288810} :answer-field/id #uuid "09418823-c229-5178-abcd-3466929f3782" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289745}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0430c7a-d01d-554d-86e5-a8ca9cccf773" :question/math-academy-id "q-211462" :question/problem "![](images/80/8000655c1943c60282d8844a2ae965b6415e21962c44309cd68806f04744b806.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global maximum of $y=-f(2x)+3?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=-f(2x)+3$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis,\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis, and finally\n\n- translating it by $3$ units up.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global **minimum** of $y=f(x)$ to the global **maximum** of $y=-f(2x)+3.$ So, we need to find the image of $(2,3),$ the global minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-f(2x)+3,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,3).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph  along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ and get the graph of $y=f(2x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2,3)=(1,3).\n$$\n\n- Next, we flip the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(2x).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,-1\\cdot 3)=(1,-3).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we translate the result by $3$ units up and get the graph of $y=-f(2x)+3.$ After this transformation, the point $(1,-3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,-3+3)=(1,0).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global maximum of $y=-f(2x)+3$ is $(1,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290288 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22806c5a-b11e-585d-8a0f-a07b37da8cf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288252} {:answer/id #uuid "45804e6e-0d5a-5c5c-8ca9-ac69387a3a42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288504} {:answer/id #uuid "4c804f40-e7dd-5a93-ba9e-361b241596a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288561} {:answer/id #uuid "a09c844e-4df9-5435-b6c7-7f385f6c6b4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289103} {:answer/id #uuid "e59fe59e-da66-54f1-9fec-c264288861a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c804f40-e7dd-5a93-ba9e-361b241596a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288561} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bbb8ab9-7f8a-598e-bca8-05a19db1385b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289859}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "064518a2-d16c-5c0a-a491-772f5bec85b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-211465" :question/problem "![](images/1e/1eb29eb3eb3627e24a23d7315295a1d889308d67895f9b798dbf9dd71ba226df.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local minimum of \n$y=-f(x-1)-2?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=-f(x-1)-2$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $1$ unit to the right, then\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis, and finally\n\n- translating it by $2$ units down.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local **maximum** of $y=f(x)$ to the local **minimum** of $y=-f(x-1)-2.$ So, we need to find the image of $(2,3),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-f(x-1)-2,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,3).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the right and get the graph of $y=f(x-1).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2+1,3)=(3,3).\n$$\n\n- Next, we flip the result vertically across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x-1).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-1\\cdot 3)=(3,-3).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the graph vertically by $2$ units down and get the graph of $y=-f(x-1)-2.$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-3-2)=(3,-5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local minimum of $y=-f(x-1)-2$ is $(3,-5).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290289 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a28ee08-5e5f-527a-adc3-f3890854472b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288432} {:answer/id #uuid "838017c7-1d24-5ee3-ac34-a0db795b7d8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288916} {:answer/id #uuid "9089b6ef-eeb8-5031-bd43-fa7c41df89e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289000} {:answer/id #uuid "99e0e358-63cd-5258-bfe8-04da80c3c6ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289059} {:answer/id #uuid "c982cb7f-975b-5883-b586-8a2935b20c43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9089b6ef-eeb8-5031-bd43-fa7c41df89e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289000} :answer-field/id #uuid "9547c087-63db-52ee-8b78-af1f430f6c34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290065}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b60ced4-eb21-5887-aa63-3d04549eb22d" :question/math-academy-id "q-211466" :question/problem "![](images/2d/2d046da9a89fe09f09dbd5e0658422ab8ccee8db680a2955ebe1ff0f361b5c35.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of \n$y=-4f(3x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=-4f(3x)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis, then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis, and finally\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global **maximum** of $y=f(x)$ to the global **minimum** of $y=-4f(3x).$ So, we need to find the image of $(3,1),$ the global maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-4f(3x),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(3,1).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph  along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ and get the graph of $y=f(3x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3,1)=(1,1).\n$$\n\n- Next, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $4$ along the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=4f(3x).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,4\\cdot 1)=(1,4).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we flip the result vertically across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-4f(3x).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,-1\\cdot 4)=(1,-4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=-4f(3x)$ is $(1,-4).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290290 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2413608e-a3a6-590f-9e1e-897ac8dc08c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288261} {:answer/id #uuid "2d3a9c3e-29ce-572f-b752-3c912b43b9c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288330} {:answer/id #uuid "e22ce0bf-657a-50a0-8cb3-437c8db17017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289539} {:answer/id #uuid "ef97605c-5e46-5c1a-a15a-28db7c637985" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289614} {:answer/id #uuid "fedf6ff2-ece5-5b19-8e86-c049a3043220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e22ce0bf-657a-50a0-8cb3-437c8db17017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289539} :answer-field/id #uuid "75c728d2-d1e5-5451-a8fc-2171c08fe3ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289987}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "633e6d77-b78e-5137-95dd-2fdc7f61a086" :question/math-academy-id "q-211467" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d8d23cf36bc9fb526c0e6d041b72e6b378638f662b67aae63531c66b4a8cd6c5.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=-f(x+2)+4?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=-f(x+2)+4$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $2$ units to the left, then\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis, and finally\n\n- translating it by $4$ units up.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local **minimum** of $y=f(x)$ to the local **maximum** of $y=-f(x+2)+4.$ So, we need to find the image of $(1,-4),$ the local minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-f(x+2)+4,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(1,-4).$\n\n- Then, we translate the graph by $2$ units to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x+2).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1-2,-4)=(-1,-4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we flip the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x+2).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,-1\\cdot (-4))=(-1,4).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we translate the result by $4$ units up and get the graph of $y=-f(x+2)+4.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,4+4)=(-1,8).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=-f(x+2)+4$ is $(-1,8).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema on Transformed Curves"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059710 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059523 :question/id #uuid "1d2717f9-b905-59eb-85d7-6db4f9c9dc7f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4292" :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}$ if $\\mathbf{a}=-4\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=12\\mathbf{i}$." :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}$, we just need to compute all of the $\\mathbf{i}$'s and $\\mathbf{j}$'s, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na - 2b &= (-4i + 6j) - 2(12i) \\\\\n&=-4i + 6j - 24i \\\\\n&= (-4i - 24i) + 6j \\\\\n&=-28i + 6j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d63a46dd-b963-5ecc-a96d-e0204ca480a9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048816 :topic/math-academy-id 1165 :topic/title "Two-Dimensional Vectors Expressed in Component Form"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060392 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8091513a-8f2f-5ec9-9cf7-c2d508bd8c12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10i + 4j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058975} {:answer/id #uuid "87ad0660-67f4-53a4-9f17-5dc2909a9981" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4i - 8j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058976} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd76fd5-06e8-5e9a-968c-ffff7b934e62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4i - 4j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058977} {:answer/id #uuid "4af2f7e3-95fd-592d-b14e-b329daf8d287" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10i - 4j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058978} {:answer/id #uuid "7ffb53e9-fff6-5a11-a761-7671492d0bb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4i + 4j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058979}] :answer-field/id #uuid "65a33916-dac4-5621-9a49-f57851b3ad93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058974}] :question/id #uuid "2d0f8d02-fc5f-57a9-b1d0-9905d55d3bce" :question/math-academy-id "q-70401" :question/problem "Find $a + b$ if $a = 7i - 6j$ and $b = 3i + 2j$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060393 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "819bbd77-81fe-5b0c-9709-5c20913178d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i - 2j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058981} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c53f60-402e-51b4-9ae0-6ebe0157d93b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058982} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f501e7-842b-5916-8760-d05182b0f466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14i + 2j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058983} {:answer/id #uuid "05b0b519-c4c6-5a48-bc7f-81176270be84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058984} {:answer/id #uuid "2e1600f0-3fc1-5dad-bc64-a8c5fa92fbb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14i - 2j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058985}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6e77c6a2-22c0-59a3-b06d-8d99ffe45d98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058980}] :question/id #uuid "0e61282c-5ba8-52b2-b8ef-94b1e6e60711" :question/math-academy-id "q-70402" :question/problem "Find $2a + b$ if $a = 4i + 2j$ and $b = 6i - 4j$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233364 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3a558acd-0b05-5aa8-b518-45323798923a" :question/math-academy-id "q-125086" :question/problem "Find $a+3b$ if $a=8i-5j$ and $b=-3i+2j.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $a+3b,$ we just need to compute all of the $i$'s and $j$'s, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+3b & =(8i-5j)+3(-3i+2j) \\\\  & =8i-5j-9i+6j \\\\  & =(8i-9i)+(-5j+6j) \\\\  & =-i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233365 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d15383e5-4c36-54f6-9a69-8b61467023e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-125088" :question/problem "Find $3a-2b$ if $a=-2i-2j$ and $b=4i-3j.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $3a-2b,$ we just need to compute all of the $i$'s and $j$'s, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a-2b & =3(-2i-2j)-2(4i-3j) \\\\  & =-6i-6j-8i+6j \\\\  & =(-6i-8i)+(-6j+6j) \\\\  & =-14i+0j \\\\  & =-14i\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Addition and Scalar Multiplication of Vectors in Component Form"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059711 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059524 :question/id #uuid "2cb63c87-320a-5cb4-b413-8c6bf9723641" :question/math-academy-id "e-4293" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -7, 15 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 14, -11 \\rangle$. Find $\\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b}$." :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b}$, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na - 3b &= ⟨ - 7, 15⟩ - 3 \\cdot ⟨14,-11⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 7, 15⟩ - ⟨42,-33⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 7 - 42, 15 - (-33)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 49, 48⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1735c445-9c97-5aa7-83d6-7dee23993153" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060025 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a296d905-1c52-5d9f-b51e-5bf51035ecb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3, 3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056509} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7f554b-907a-5453-a719-30b2d5927f41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨21, 3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056510} {:answer/id #uuid "deb9cb96-aa10-543b-8e06-8e68021d3bdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨21, 7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056511} {:answer/id #uuid "5aafc1a4-0f99-5fba-91be-0462a2cfbd51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨ - 3, 7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056512} {:answer/id #uuid "62c580e7-f5e8-5469-bbe8-7cb30c672690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3, 7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056513}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3fcc4fd2-1467-564e-9c21-a9a49176b8d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056508}] :question/id #uuid "b1d8d19e-dfcf-542f-877a-8cdb7c9e0749" :question/math-academy-id "q-1304" :question/problem "If $u = ⟨12, 5⟩$ and $v = ⟨9,-2⟩$, then $u - v =$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060118 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "850889de-d865-5b67-96be-c9b833da9e